@page "/common-pages"

<CatHead Title="Common pages" Tier="@CatHead.Tier1">
    The pages every app has and nobody designs: access denied, not found, the thing that threw,
    an expired session, a maintenance window. Each is a shell from
    <a href="/frame">Shell</a> with one <code>.empty-state--page</code> block in it.
</CatHead>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-information-line"></i>
    <strong>These are compositions, not new classes.</strong> A page-level state is the same block
    an empty panel uses, given the page instead of a card — so an app that has styled its empty
    states has already styled these. Copy the markup; do not restyle it per project.
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.CommonPages.AccessDenied" Title="Access denied" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.auth-layout</code>, because there is no navigation to offer somebody who cannot get
        in — a sidebar behind a 403 is a list of links that will all do this again.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Say who can grant it.</strong> &ldquo;You do not have permission&rdquo; on its own
        leaves the reader with nowhere to go, and the next thing they do is open a ticket against
        the wrong team. Name the role and the person who holds it, and make asking for it the
        primary action.
    </p>
    <p>
        The account line belongs in <code>.auth-foot</code>. A 403 is often the wrong account
        rather than a missing role, and the reader cannot tell without being told which one they
        are signed in as.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.CommonPages.NotFound" Title="Not found" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        The one that keeps the frame. The reader is signed in and the nav still works, so
        <code>.layout</code> stays and the block goes in <code>.page</code> — dropping to
        <code>.auth-layout</code> here would take away the way out.
    </p>
    <p>
        In <code>.page</code> the block sits at the top rather than centring, because
        <code>.page</code> is a block and <code>flex: 1</code> is inert in one. That is the right
        result: a message centred in a scroll container lands below the fold on a laptop.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Name the thing that was not found.</strong> The reference is what the reader will
        paste into a search or quote to a colleague, and it is the only part of a 404 that is worth
        anything.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.CommonPages.ServerError" Title="Something went wrong" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.empty-state--failed</code> colours the disc and nothing else. A whole red page reads
        as destructive — as though something was lost — when what happened is that a request did not
        arrive.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Two things make this page useful, and neither is the apology.</strong> Say whether
        retrying is safe, because the reader cannot know whether the write went through. And give
        them the reference in <code>.empty-state-detail</code>, with a
        <code>data-copy</code> button beside it — that string is what turns a support call into a
        log lookup.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.CommonPages.SessionExpired" Title="Session expired" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        For the hard redirect. Where the app is still running and only the circuit dropped, this is
        the <a href="/status-bar"><code>.status-bar</code></a>'s job instead — a whole page for a
        reconnect that will succeed in two seconds throws away the state the reader had.
    </p>
    <p>
        Say what happened to the work in progress. An expired session reads as lost work until told
        otherwise, and the reader will redo something they did not need to.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.CommonPages.Maintenance" Title="Maintenance" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>The title is the time it comes back</strong>, not the word
        &ldquo;maintenance&rdquo;. That is the one fact the reader came for, and every minute they
        do not have it is a minute they spend refreshing.
    </p>
    <p>
        Give a window and a way to check, and keep the same page for the planned and unplanned
        cases. A separate &ldquo;unexpected outage&rdquo; design is a page that gets written once
        and looked at during the worst hour of the quarter.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="which-shell">Which shell, which drawing</h2>

<p>
    Each of these has its own drawing in the state sprite, and the drawing is chosen by what the page
    is. Reusing one across two of them makes them look like the same page to somebody scanning a
    screenshot in a ticket — which is the whole reason there are thirteen and not one ornament.
    <a href="/empty-state#state-art">The full set is on Empty and live states</a>.
</p>

<table class="table">
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Page</th><th>Shell</th><th>Drawing</th><th>Primary action</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Access denied</td><td><code>.auth-layout</code></td><td><code>#no-access</code></td><td>Request the role</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Not found</td><td><code>.layout</code> &rarr; <code>.page</code></td><td><code>#not-found</code></td><td>Search for the reference</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Server error</td><td><code>.auth-layout</code></td><td><code>#server-error</code></td><td>Try again</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Session expired</td><td><code>.auth-layout</code></td><td><code>#session-expired</code></td><td>Sign in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maintenance</td><td><code>.auth-layout</code></td><td><code>#maintenance</code></td><td>Check again</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p>
    Sign-in itself is not on this list because it is not a state — it is a form, and it belongs in an
    <a href="/frame"><code>.auth-card</code></a>.
</p>

<h2 id="writing-one">Writing one</h2>

<p>
    Three lines, in this order: what would normally be here, why it is not, and the one action that
    changes that. <code>.empty-state-code</code> carries the status code above the title — small and
    quotable, because what the reader needs it for is telling somebody else which page broke.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>One primary action, and at most two beside it.</strong>
    <code>.empty-state-actions</code> wraps and centres them and takes the top margin off the
    buttons, so a second action does not stagger against the first on a narrow screen. A page with
    three equal buttons is a page that does not know what the reader should do.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>The drawing is <code>.state-art--lg</code> here</strong>, the largest of the three sizes.
    The same symbol at <code>.state-art</code> is the card size and <code>.state-art--sm</code> the
    table size, so a state looks like itself wherever it appears — the 403 in a panel is the 403 on
    this page, smaller.
</p>
